@TristanSag7 You also have the choice of reviewing every single trade you made and rating it from A to C (A being a good trade which you would take regardless of win/loss)

In a sense you're still trading but taking the position of cash rather than long/short

Your trade log diligently kept is where you will find answers to profitability

@JANSEYFARTH2 @TristanSag7 One other aspect I've found helpful is definitely in revisiting the books that have inspired you!

I've lately reread Trading With The O'Neil Disciples and man, I realized there were many nuances that Gil and Chris left in there that I missed out!

The other is to deep dive a trader in an attempt to 'get into their head'. For example, someone like Dan Zanger archives all his newsletters dating back to 1998 - it could be quite instructive to read through and chart out the stocks he has mentioned (whereever possible) to live through those market environments again - could be an interesting exercise!

@TedHZhang @bot Computer use is the worst-case scenario: the agent has to be good at using a computer, logging in, browsing, and navigating. 

What's exciting is that more and more companies will become agent-ready. An agent can go to an LLM's .txt file or MCP that exists and communicate with a service or a company that way, rather than using the good old computer use that humans have been doing for a while. 

As more and more companies become agent-ready, this tech will become more and more seamless, and the more seamless it becomes, the more magical it will feel
